A||||4||maryjane||June 6-57 yrs ago- sorta long||||1||
Z||000000||maryjane||06-06-2001||10:41 PM||||I got up this morn & turned on the tv -saw the date on a news show and instantly knew the significance of this day. We all go thru our daily routines, our little problems(or big ones).We love and hate our Fieros,wives,girlfriends,families,jobs,personal situations & they all seem like major things to us. 57 years ago today, before most Americans had awakened, thousands had been killed or wounded on beaches with names like Omaha,Gold,Sword,Juneau. Not just Americans,Canadians,British,French and a lot more. I'm a VietNam vet, but it aint a big deal to me anymore,it's over and done with. I don't travel much anymore either, but if I wanted to drive from Texas to The Dells or Carlisle, I'm free to do so without any checkpoints or too much hassle. Anyway, I just got to thinkin how terribly hellish those beaches must have been for those men,<BR>most of em as young as I was in VietNam.(19)<BR>We have it damn good in this world today,our problems aren't much in comparison to back then. Everything we are and ever hope to be we owe to those who fought in wwII. They literally bought our freedom for us, and it was a awful high price to pay. Just hope we never forget it. Well I'll climb down off my soapbox now, just a few thoughts I wanted to say to somebody,anybody.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||06-06-2001||10:56 PM||||You're absolutely right. <BR>I wasn't around then. Was too young for 'Nam. Was too old for Desert Storm. But don't think I'm not grateful to all who fought for our country, over the years. <BR>My father was a tank mechanic in France during the last part (I think) of WWII. He never did talk much about it, though. <BR>About the only way I can relate is by having seen Saving Private Ryan. I only wish he would have lived long enough (died in 88) to have seen it with me. I thought it was a fitting tribute.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||Tweek||06-07-2001||12:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>...I don't travel much anymore either, but if I wanted to drive from Texas to The Dells or Carlisle, I'm free to do so without any checkpoints or too much hassle... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Luckily, if you throw enough quarters at em, they dont say too much..  =)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||06-07-2001||09:55 AM||||I agree completely.  Our freedom has never been bought once.  It has been paid for time and time again, from the Revolutionary War, WWI, WWII, etc.  Detractors may say Desert Storm wasn't about our freedom, and I won't debate that here.  My father served in both WWII and Korea.  A close friend of mine served in Desert Storm.  I salute all service people for the sacrifices they made so that I might enjoy the freedom I have today.  That's why it's my tradition, on Memorial Day, to go to a local bar that I frequent and silently raise a glass in honor of those service people.  <P>Thank you all.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||06-07-2001||11:16 PM||||Well said Don!<BR>U.S. Army Retired, 22 yrs. 2 mos. 2 days.<BR>UH-1H Senior Crew non rated.<BR>God Bless All Who Served!||||reg||1
